Description: The camera hack's separation from the person's depth limits, a still canvas drawn with both cameras, the scene's plane (ORDER 473t, 473u)
 The camera hack drew a canvas that is drawn once out of view with one camera, put the page's camera between the two eyes (so a
 2D output showed a picture half a separation off the page's own), kept the zero-parallax plane at five times the near distance
 whatever the scene, and used a fixed 2 percent separation whatever the person's depth limits said. The host now draws two views
 from the first draw, the left eye is the page's own camera (webgl.stereo-camera.left-eye-is-page-camera), the plane follows
 the nearest and the farthest thing in the depth buffer inside the person's popped limit (webgl.stereo-camera.scene-plane, and
 dom.vr.flat.scene-plane for the flat WebXR display), the separation is the sunk limit of the window's kde_stereo_depth_v1
 depth_limits (a point at infinity lands at that sunk limit in the output's pixels whatever the canvas's width) unscaled by any choice of the browser, and a depth buffer that cannot be read is said once on the page's web console.
Author: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Forwarded: not-needed
Assisted-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 via Claude Code
